<section class="band band--sand">
  <div class="shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">Not four separate purchases</p>
    <h2>Why these belong together</h2>
    <p class="intro-wide">Most therapists assemble this from four providers who have never met
    and who know nothing about each other's work with you: a supervisor for the hours, someone
    else for EMDR consultation, a vendor for the ethics requirement, and a retreat if there is
    time and money left. Here it is one relationship, and that changes what each part can do.</p>
    <div class="cards">
      <div>
        <h3>The EMDR consultation is included</h3>
        <p>Charged by the hour on its own. Free while you are supervised here, so hours toward
        EMDRIA certification build alongside your 3,000 rather than after them.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>The ethics hours fill the half that is hard to fill</h3>
        <p>Since 2024, half of your 24 CE hours must come from a provider listed in 22 TAC
        &sect;681.140(f). Subsection (f)(6) is a licensee with supervisor status, which Reem
        holds.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>One lens, not four curricula</h3>
        <p>NARM, Somatic Experiencing and IFS-informed EMDR frame all of it: the supervision
        hour, the consultation hour, the trail and the CEU. What you learn in one is usable in
        the next.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>It spans the career, not the credential</h3>
        <p>Supervision ends at licensure. Certification, ethics hours and your own depletion do
        not. Staying means working with someone who already knows how you think under
        pressure.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    {% bandArt "/assets/img/reem-and-blu.jpg", 758, 397, "Reem Glasco standing beside Blu, a bay horse, holding a lead rope in a sandy arena." %}
    <p class="caption">Equine-assisted supervision runs near Dripping Springs, $60 a session,
    optional.</p>
    {% include "partials/cta-trio.njk" %}
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band band--night">
  <div class="shell">
    <h2>Who tends to work well here</h2>
    <div class="cards">
      <div>
        <h3>The associate who wants more than hours</h3>
        <p>You could get cheaper supervision. What you want is someone who will tell you what
        actually happened in that session, and help you build something after licensure.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>The therapist whose EMDR keeps stalling</h3>
        <p>Preparation looks fine, then processing floods or flatlines. Usually that is a part
        of the client objecting, and it is workable once you can see it.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>The clinician running on empty</h3>
        <p>Competent, respected, quietly depleted. You teach regulation skills you no longer
        practice. That is what the retreats are for.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>The one drawn to body-based work</h3>
        <p>You have read about somatic approaches and want to practice inside one, with a
        supervisor who works that way every week rather than recommending it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="intro-wide">More on what that means in the hour itself:
    <a href="/blog/trauma-informed-supervision/">what makes supervision trauma-informed</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

